# o is two vowels, and this is the half of them the writing does say.
#
# Catalan has an open o and a close one and the letter is the same for both:
# groc is [g r O k] and tot is [t o t]. Where the word carries a grave the
# rule above settles it, and where it does not the writing is silent -- which
# is what the grammars say too, and why the accent exists. So there is no
# reading of the letter alone that gets it right, and what can be had is the
# contexts that are near enough exceptionless.
#
# This is the first of them, and the strongest: an o with a u or an i after it
# is open. That is the diphthong -- nou, bou, prou, plou, dijous, noia, boira,
# coure, sou -- and the grammars give it as a rule rather than a tendency.
# The other contexts, the endings, are the arm below it.
#
# The scan is set rightwards from the right-hand end, which is the letter
# after the o, exactly as the stop rules ask whether a word has ended.
rule apply_span_o_rules takes 3 from st_phone.obj
  local left bytes 8
  local right bytes 8
  call ZZget_parm_ptr2 addr left arg 1 addr right arg 2
  call ZZfenceZZstring109
  plant test ending
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 addr right
  if answer is 0
    call cat_glide_letter                                  # a u or an i?
    if answer is 0
      call cat_insert_oopen addr left addr right
      if answer is 0
        go to said
      end
    end
  end
  backtrack
place ending
  # And the endings. An o before a c or an l that the word ends on is open --
  # groc, poc, foc, joc; sol, vol, col, gol -- and so is the same o before the
  # feminine and the plural, which is escola, escoles and bola. glob.up says
  # which consonants are in and why the rest of the grammars' list is out.
  plant test word_list
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 addr right
